// The human's movement: game/human_locomotion.cpp linked as it is, with the real collision world (game/collision*.cpp)
// on ground of my own (collision/synth_level.inc), the LS3DF port's math and the fake engine (common/fake_engine.inc).
// MinGW; build.bat; run human_test.exe ("human: N checks, 0 failures").
//
// Every call is compared with the original executable's (collision/original_exe.h): the port's objects are copied into
// the original's layout (human_layouts.inc, generated by _re/tools/layout_offsets.py from the headers' offset
// comments; the locomotion, the mixer, the work and the other parts of the movement have the original's layout
// already), the original's function runs on that copy, and its result is compared with a copy taken of the port's
// after its own call. What the locomotion calls outside itself is faked on both sides alike (the port's functions
// defined here, the original's redirected to the same loggers): each call is logged with its arguments and the logs
// must agree; what they return is scripted (S_Script). The original's own small helpers (AngleBetween2D, ...) serve
// both sides.
//
// Part 1 (locomotion_test.inc): on the ground: MoveOnGround with its sweeps (SweepBody), slopes (SlowOnSlope,
// AdjustToGround), falls (UpdateFall, AccelerateFall, FallDistance), landing and fall damage (Land, ApplyFallDamage),
// the ground contact of a sweep (FindGroundContact).
